    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-77219",
      "knowledge_point_id": "1e310cbe-9d6b-5bc4-960a-8813333bc8be",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Arc Length of a Planar Curve",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081725/assets/2324d20426a6ef6110714341f256ed3943c66d4068cdb9bd3d1763f8f528f5a1.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨6t,-\\frac{3}{t}-{t}^{3}⟩$\nfrom  $t=1$ to $t=3.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{t}-{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}-3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}-3{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{4+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}-2+{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}+2+{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}3(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{3}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3(-\\frac{1}{t}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{t}+{t}^{3}){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(-1+27)-(-3+1) \\\\  & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
